WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.680 --> 00:00:05.009
Few DC Comics fans could have predicted a
few years ago that Warner Bros. would release

00:00:05.009 --> 00:00:11.080
a new Batman movie in 2022 that is not only
set outside of the DC Extended Universe but

00:00:11.080 --> 00:00:15.120
also stars someone other than Ben Affleck
as the Caped Crusader.

00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:17.680
However, that's exactly what has happened.

00:00:17.680 --> 00:00:22.039
Now, after entrusting "War for the Planet
of the Apes" director Matt Reeves with its

00:00:22.039 --> 00:00:28.449
most coveted superhero property, WB has finally
unveiled the Robert Pattinson-led "The Batman"

00:00:28.449 --> 00:00:30.910
for everyone to see.

00:00:30.910 --> 00:00:35.430
Taking inspiration from multiple different
comic book and cinematic sources, "The Batman"

00:00:35.430 --> 00:00:38.680
isn't like a lot of the other comic book movies
that have come before it.

00:00:38.680 --> 00:00:43.310
While the film adopts the same kind of grounded,
gritty aesthetic that dominated Christopher

00:00:43.310 --> 00:00:48.480
Nolan's Dark Knight trilogy, "The Batman"
goes even further with that style.

00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:53.050
The movie also leans harder into the detective
side of its titular character, delivering

00:00:53.050 --> 00:00:57.940
a movie that is not only a superhero flick
but also a thrilling and genuinely intriguing

00:00:57.940 --> 00:00:59.120
crime mystery.

00:00:59.120 --> 00:01:04.199
Thanks to the presence of its primary villain,
Edward Nashton AKA The Riddler, "The Batman"

00:01:04.199 --> 00:01:07.290
also gets incredibly dark at certain points.

00:01:07.290 --> 00:01:11.820
As a matter of fact, it may very well be the
darkest live-action Batman movie that comic

00:01:11.820 --> 00:01:13.810
book fans have ever seen.

00:01:13.810 --> 00:01:17.340
Batfans beware, spoilers ahead!

00:01:17.340 --> 00:01:21.310
From its opening scene, "The Batman" sets
Paul Dano's Riddler up to be the scariest

00:01:21.310 --> 00:01:24.950
live-action villain in Batman movie history.

00:01:24.950 --> 00:01:29.720
Opening with one long, voyeuristic shot from
the character's POV, the film's first sequence

00:01:29.720 --> 00:01:34.280
sees The Riddler break into the home of Gotham
City's mayor on Halloween night and brutally

00:01:34.280 --> 00:01:37.909
kill the man while his family members are
out trick-or-treating.

00:01:37.909 --> 00:01:42.119
The sequence is unexpectedly dark and creepy,
but it's only the first of the film's many

00:01:42.119 --> 00:01:44.890
impactful and unsettling Riddler scenes.

00:01:44.890 --> 00:01:49.431
Indeed, over the course of its nearly 3-hour
runtime, "The Batman" follows The Riddler

00:01:49.431 --> 00:01:54.590
as he goes on a bloody and gruesome crusade,
killing many of Gotham City's most powerful

00:01:54.590 --> 00:01:57.729
and corrupt law enforcement officials.

00:01:57.729 --> 00:02:02.040
With each of his kills, The Riddler reveals
a little bit more about himself and the relationship

00:02:02.040 --> 00:02:04.649
that he thinks he has with Pattinson's Batman.

00:02:04.649 --> 00:02:09.409
However, his main mission in "The Batman"
is to expose the corruption that has run rampant

00:02:09.409 --> 00:02:13.760
throughout Gotham for decades by killing the
people who allowed the city's citizens to

00:02:13.760 --> 00:02:18.139
be taken advantage of, lied to, and kept in
the dark.

00:02:18.139 --> 00:02:22.840
As a result, the character, who was partially
inspired by terrifying real-life figures like

00:02:22.840 --> 00:02:27.230
the Zodiac Killer, sets out to send a different
message with each of the crimes scenes he

00:02:27.230 --> 00:02:29.010
leaves behind.

00:02:29.010 --> 00:02:32.859
With that in mind, it's worth asking: Which
character in "The Batman" experiences the

00:02:32.859 --> 00:02:35.120
most gruesome death?

00:02:35.120 --> 00:02:39.370
Due to the nature of his mission in "The Batman,"
The Riddler leaves a surprisingly high number

00:02:39.370 --> 00:02:41.079
of victims in his wake.

00:02:41.079 --> 00:02:45.379
Indeed, the villain performs several heinous
acts throughout the movie, including strapping

00:02:45.379 --> 00:02:50.150
a bomb to the neck of Gil Colson, Gotham City's
corrupt district attorney, at one point in

00:02:50.150 --> 00:02:51.269
its second act.

00:02:51.269 --> 00:02:55.569
That said, the most gruesome death in "The
Batman" is actually the one that fans see

00:02:55.569 --> 00:02:59.900
the least of in the film itself, and that's
the murder of corrupt police commissioner

00:02:59.900 --> 00:03:01.549
Pete Savage.

00:03:01.549 --> 00:03:05.780
Introduced near the tail end of the film's
first crime scene sequence, Savage is initially

00:03:05.780 --> 00:03:10.799
set up to be a major thorn in the sides of
both James Gordon and Batman.

00:03:10.799 --> 00:03:15.400
However, Savage's presence in "The Batman"
comes to a quick and unexpected end early

00:03:15.400 --> 00:03:19.779
on in the film when he is revealed to be the
second of The Riddler's victims.

00:03:19.779 --> 00:03:23.939
Unlike most of the deaths in "The Batman,"
Savage's demise isn't shown on-screen when

00:03:23.939 --> 00:03:25.040
it happens.

00:03:25.040 --> 00:03:29.439
Instead, the character's death is revealed
in a news report, which briefly shows a video

00:03:29.439 --> 00:03:32.989
that The Riddler recorded during Savage's
last moments.

00:03:32.989 --> 00:03:37.829
In it, the audience briefly sees Savage sitting
with a complex trap strapped across his chest

00:03:37.829 --> 00:03:39.439
and face.

00:03:39.439 --> 00:03:43.739
Later in the film, it is revealed that The
Riddler injected Savage with rat poison and

00:03:43.739 --> 00:03:47.969
then placed rats in the trap on his body,
which eventually chewed and burrowed into

00:03:47.969 --> 00:03:49.629
his face and neck.

00:03:49.629 --> 00:03:54.579
The complex, depraved, and drawn-out nature
of Savage's death is what makes it truly horrifying

00:03:54.579 --> 00:03:59.829
to think about, and that's also why "The Batman"
ultimately shows very little of it and only

00:03:59.829 --> 00:04:05.469
briefly alludes to the state of Savage's body
after he is discovered by his police colleagues.

